{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>You shall not eat upon the blood.</b> Joined with the verse above, for it warned about a planted tree that they shall not eat its fruit until the fifth year; likewise, any clean flesh shall not be eaten until its blood is thrown upon the altar of HaShem, if it was near the place of holiness. And the trustworthy witness is the words of Saul, for the Ark was with him, as it is written. Now, eating upon the blood is as though eating sacrifices to goat-demons, as I have explained, for such was their practice in Egypt — to slaughter in the name of the demons. For since the blood was not thrown upon the altar for the name of HaShem, the matter is clear. Therefore it is joined with it: 'You shall not divine' — for in Egypt they would eat upon the blood and go astray after the demons, and in the land of Canaan they divine and practice augury, as it is written: 'after the doings of the land of Egypt' and 'after the doings of the land of Canaan.' The word נחש means testing, like 'I will put a spell' (נחשתי) — 'and he will bless me' — for they divine with forms and with staffs and with actions and with movements and with days and hours." "<b>You shall not practice augury.</b> Some say it is from the root ענה, that one thinks a thing in his heart and inclines his ear to hear what the speaker will answer, but the grammatical analysis does not accept this. Some say עונן is from the root of 'and she answered' (ועונתה) — this should not be diminished. The correct view in my eyes is from the root ענן, for it is known that there are those who practice augury by looking at the clouds, at their forms and their movements. And it is possible that תעוננו is a doubled-form (פעלי הכפל) like תסובבו, for we find 'and augurers' (ועוננים) like the Philistines — for it can be a doubled stem (בנין הכפול); the proof: 'a diviner and an augurer' (ומעונן ומנחש)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The phrase 'כי נחוש' alludes to Genesis 30:27 where Laban says 'נחשתי ויברכני יהוה'; Ibn Ezra compresses the quotation, omitting 'and HaShem has blessed me.' The word 'שעירים' (goat-demons) refers back to Leviticus 17:7.", 1 "The final proof citation 'ומעונן ומנחש' appears to be from the reverse of the phrase in 1 Samuel 6:2, which reads 'ומנחשים ומעוננים'; Ibn Ezra may be quoting from memory or a variant tradition."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:11:22.932131420Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}